      "text": "To determine the maximum number of planes that can be formed from 12 points in space, we need to calculate the number of combinations of 3 points out of 12. This is given by the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{12}{3} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose 3 points from 12 without regard to the order of selection.\n\nThe formula for the binomial coefficient is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nFor our problem, \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( r = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling the \\( 9! \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6}\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\( 12 \\times 11 \\):\n\n\\[\n12 \\times 11 = 132\n\\]\n\nThen, multiply by 10:\n\n\\[\n132 \\times 10 = 1320\n\\]\n\nNext, divide by 6:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nHence, the maximum number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]",
